            <ArticleTitle>[Is the quality of off-pump coronary artery bypass grafting equivalent to that of conventional coronary artery bypass grafting].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>699-702</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We analyzed 44 patients undergoing coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG) from November 2001 to March 2003. Of 44 patients, 40 patients (29 men and 11 women; mean age 68.1 +/- 9.2 years) underwent off-pump CABG (OPCAB) [90.9%]. The mean number of bypass grafts was 2.53 +/- 0.78. In-hospital mortality was none and major complications except for one case of reexploration for bleeding were not seen. The early patency rate of over-all grafts was 94.6%. The quality of OPCAB was considered to be equivalent to conventional CABG.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
